model.en:
Define a typed error and chain the original with { cause }:

class ConfigError extends Error {
  constructor(message, options) {
    super(message, options); // forwards { cause }
    this.name = 'ConfigError';
  }
}

async function loadConfig(path) {
  try {
    return JSON.parse(await readFile(path, 'utf8'));
  } catch (err) {
    throw new ConfigError(`config load failed: ${path}`, { cause: err });
  }
}

Why it's right: the caller can branch on `instanceof ConfigError` (or err.name) for a clean API, while err.cause still holds the original error — whether it's an ENOENT from readFile or a SyntaxError from JSON.parse — with its full stack. A logger or util.inspect prints the whole cause chain, so you keep both a meaningful type and the root-cause trace. Setting this.name ensures logs and instanceof read correctly.

